Implement smulhi/umulhi on i64 in the CLIF-to-wasm translator

`parse::<i64>()` and `parse::<u64>()` do not compile with this backend.
`core::num::from_ascii_radix` accumulates digits with `checked_mul`,
Cranelift emits `smulhi.i64` for the overflow half, and the translator's
binary-opcode table maps one CLIF opcode to one wasm operator — so an
opcode needing a sequence falls through to:

    unimplemented binary opcode smulhi on i64

WebAssembly has no high-half multiply, but the translator already builds
one: `i128_lower::mul` computes `umulhi64` inline from 32-bit partial
products to get its own high word. This lifts that out as a helper, uses
it from both places, and adds the signed correction

    smulhi(a, b) = umulhi(a, b) - ((a >> 63) & b) - ((b >> 63) & a)

An arithmetic shift by 63 is all-ones when the operand is negative and
zero otherwise, so each term subtracts the other operand exactly when
this one is negative.

Verified by assembling the emitted sequence into a WebAssembly module and
comparing against exact 128-bit arithmetic over i64::MIN, i64::MAX, their
neighbours, the powers of two either side of the 32-bit split, every step
of an i64 decimal parse in both signs, and 500,000 random pairs:
1,001,998 comparisons, no disagreements.

